# Ambient Fast Synthesis and Active Sites Deciphering of Hierarchical Foam‐Like Trimetal–Organic Framework Nanostructures as a Platform for Highly Efficient Oxygen Evolution Electrocatalysis

## abstract
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have attracted tremendous interest due to their promising applications including electrocatalysis originating from their unique structural features. However, it remains a challenge to directly use MOFs for oxygen electrocatalysis because it is quite difficult to manipulate their dimension, composition, and morphology of the MOFs with abundant active sites. Here, a facile ambient temperature synthesis of unique NiCoFe-based trimetallic MOF nanostructures with foam-like architecture is reported, which exhibit extraordinary oxygen evolution reaction (OER) activity as directly used catalyst in alkaline condition. Specifically, the (Ni2 Co1 )0.925 Fe0.075 -MOF-NF delivers a minimum overpotential of 257 mV to reach the current density of 10 mA cm-2 with a small Tafel slope of 41.3 mV dec-1 and exhibits high durability after long-term testing. More importantly, the deciphering of the possible origination of the high activity is performed through the characterization of the intermediates during the OER process, where the electrochemically transformed metal hydroxides and oxyhydroxides are confirmed as the active species.

Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) have attracted tremendous interest due to their promising applications including electrocatalysis originating from their unique structural features. However, it remains a challenge to directly use MOFs for oxygen electrocatalysis because it is quite difficult to manipulate their dimension, composition, and morphology of the MOFs with abundant active sites. Here, a facile ambient temperature synthesis of unique NiCoFe-based trimetallic MOF nanostructures with foam-like architecture is reported, which exhibit extraordinary oxygen evolution reaction (OER) activity as directly used catalyst in alkaline condition. Specifically, the $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{0.925}\mathsf{F e}_{0.075}$ -MOF-NF delivers a minimum overpotential of $257\boldsymbol{\mathsf{m}}\boldsymbol{\mathsf{v}}$ to reach the current density of $\mathsf{10m A c m^{-2}}$ with a small Tafel slope of $47.3~\mathsf{m V}\mathsf{d e c}^{-1}$ and exhibits high durability after long-term testing. More importantly, the deciphering of the possible origination of the high activity is performed through the characterization of the intermediates during the OER process, where the electrochemically transformed metal hydroxides and oxyhydroxides are confirmed as the active species.
In order to solve the deteriorative environmental issues due to the rapidly increased energy consumption based on fossil fuels, there has been unprecedented desire on the development of advanced energy conversion and storage devices with both low cost and high efficiency aiming to push forward the clean and renewable energy utilization, such as fuel cells, metal–air batteries, and water electrolyzers.[1–8] However, the sluggish kinetics of oxygen evolution reaction (OER) has become the most challenging issue limiting the efficiency of these electrochemical systems because of the involved multistep proton-coupled electron transfer process from water to oxygen $(4\mathrm{OH^{-}}\rightarrow2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\ +$ $\mathrm{O}_{2}+4\mathrm{e}^{-})$ .[9,10] Currently, the commercially available noble-metal-based materials $(\mathrm{Ir}{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ , etc.) possess the state-of-theart OER electrocatalytic activity while their intrinsic disadvantages of scarcity and high-cost severely hinder their practical applications.[10–12] Therefore, it is naturally the major task to explore highly efficient yet cost effective noble metal–free OER catalysts with comparable or even better electrocatalytic activity, where tremendous efforts have been devoted during the past decade in various material systems, including transition metal–based compounds,[13–19] carbon-based materials,[20–22] and their hybrid structures.[23,24]
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are a new class of crystalline and microporous materials formed by coordination bonds between metal atom nodes and organic ligands with periodic structural units, generally possessing the unique features of tunable porosity with high specific surface areas, diversity in metal centers and functional groups, which have attracted intense attention in many fields.[25–32] In terms of electrocatalysis, it would be one of the ideal precursors for preparing unique metal compounds/carbon hybrid nanostructures via high temperature pyrolysis.[30,33–37] However, this strategy generally requires multistep synthesis, high energy–cost and the carbonization derived shrink/agglomeration, which could destroy the nanostructure and decrease the active sites. Recently, it has aroused increasing attention to the intrinsic activity of MOFs as directely used OER catalysts without extra pyrolysis treatment, where some intriguing progess has been achieved.[29,38–42] For example, Tang and co-workers discovered the high OER activity of ultrathin Ni-Co-MOF nanosheets obtained by ultrasonication exfoliation.[40] Zhang et  al. developed the electrochemical exfoliation strategy to synthesize ultrathin 2D Co-MOF structure to catalyze OER efficiently.[41] These pioneer work greatly stimulates the research interest on the design of functional MOFs which can be directly utilized as OER catalysts, while there are still some unresolved challenging issues: i) It is generally required to perform post-treatment of the as-synthesized MOFs to achieve the ultrathin nanosheet with enhanced OER activity while the controllable synthesis of MOFs with desired morphology and dimension yet high OER activity are still quite difficult. ii) In the view of practical applications, fast and large-scale synthesis under mild condition is always preferred for MOFs, where consistent contribution should be focused. iii) It is necessary yet undeveloped about the investigation on the actual active species for MOF catalyst.
Here, we presented a mild one-pot room temperature solution phase method for the large-scale synthesis of hierarchical trimetallic NiCoFe-based MOF nanofoam (denoted as $(\mathrm{Ni}_{2}\mathrm{Co}_{1})_{1-x}\mathrm{Fe}_{x}.\mathrm{MOF}.\mathrm{NF},$ ) with controlled molar ratios, for the first time, using metal acetate as precursors and 1,4-bezene­ dicarboxylate acid (1,4-BDC) as organic ligand, which can be directly used as efficient and robust OER catalysts in alkaline condition. Impressively, the as-synthesized $(\mathrm{Ni}_{2}\mathrm{Co}_{1})_{1-x}\mathrm{Fe}_{x}.$ MOF-NF delivers an extremely low overpotential of $257~\mathrm{mV}$ and a small Tafel slope of $41.3\mathrm{mV}\mathrm{dec}^{-1}$ in $1\ \mathrm{M}\ \mathrm{KOH}$ electrolyte through the facile composition modulation. More importantly, the possible active species during OER process are unraveled to be the electro­chemically transformed metal hydroxides and oxyhydroxides.
The $(\mathrm{Ni}_{2}\mathrm{Co}_{1})_{1-x}\mathrm{Fe}_{x}\mathrm{-MO}$ F-NFs are prepared via a simple and scalable one-pot room temperature solution phase method, which can be illustrated in Scheme  1. Briefly, certain amount of $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COO})_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ $\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COO})_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COO})_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ are dissolved into dimethylformamide (DMF) as the metal precursors, followed by adding stoichiometric 1, 4-BDC as the organic ligand. The atomic ratio of the $\mathrm{Ni/Co/Fe}$ in the products could be easily modulated by adjusting the amount of metal precursors. The final products are collected after $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ reaction and are directly used as OER catalysts. The detailed experimental procedures are provided in the Supporting Information.
The morphology of the $(\mathrm{Ni}_{2}\mathrm{Co}_{1})_{0.925}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.075}$ -MOF-NF is first examined by transmission electron microscopy (TEM) as shown in Figure 1A,B, where hierarchical networks composted of interconnected nanofibers can be observed to form the 3D foam-like architecture. The high-angle annular dark-field scanning transmission electron microscopy (HAADF-STEM) image and energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS) elemental mapping results (Figure  1C) demonstrate the existence and uniform distribution of Ni, Co, Fe, C, and O elements throughout the whole nanostructure. Figure  1D–F and Figures S1A–S3A (Supporting Information) show the representative TEM images for the $(\mathrm{Ni}_{2}\mathrm{Co}_{1})_{1-x}\mathrm{Fe}_{x}.$ MOF-NFs with varied Fe molar ratios of 0.025, 0.05, 0.1, 0.15, 0.175, and 0.25, respectively. As can be seen, all these samples exhibit similar foam-like hierarchical 3D nanostructures, indicating the generality of this mild room-temperature method. It should be mentioned that the addition of iron precursor plays a critical role for the formation of hierarchical foamlike architecture and only isolated nanorods can be fabricated without Fe precursor (Figure S4, Supporting Information). In order to confirm the formation of MOF structure, the X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns (Figure  1G) and the FTIR spectra (Figure S5, Supporting Information) are further collected in which the XRD patterns of the $(\mathrm{Ni}_{2}\mathrm{Co}_{1})_{1-x}\mathrm{Fe}_{x}.\mathrm{M}$ OF-NFs (with $x\leq0.075$ ) are similar to that of the simulated MOF structure (Figure S6, Supporting Information) while a small peak at around $12^{\circ}$ appears when the doping of Fe exceeds $10\%$ , indicating the possible existence of a new phase and the Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) spectra are identical to previously reported systems.[40,43,44] The formation of the $(\mathrm{Ni}_{2}\mathrm{Co}_{1})_{1-x}\mathrm{Fe}_{x}.$ MOF-NFs structure is further confirmed by the comparing X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) measurement (Figure S7, Supporting Information). The survey spectrum (Figure S7A, Supporting Information) indicates the coexistence of Ni, Co, Fe, O, and C elements while the peaks of the $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ and ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ $2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ (Figure S7B,C, Supporting Information) shift to higher binding energy in $(\mathrm{Ni}_{2}\mathrm{Co}_{1})_{1-x}\mathrm{Fe}_{x^{-}}\mathrm{MOF}.$ NFs compared with the bimetallic NiCo-based MOFs, which could stem from the partial electron transfer between $\mathrm{Ni/Co}$ and Fe,[39] further demonstrating the formation of trimetallic MOF structure.[40,45,46]
Impressively, the as-synthesized $(\mathrm{Ni}_{2}\mathrm{Co}_{1})_{1-x}\mathrm{Fe}_{x}\mathrm{-MOI}$ -NFs exhibit remarkable OER activity, which is studied with a threeelectrode system in $1.0\textrm{M K O H}$ electrolyte on glassy carbon electrode (GCE) at room temperature using graphite rod as the counter electrode with a mass loading of $0.54\mathrm{~mg~cm}^{-2}$ . Figure 2A indicates the linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) curves for the comparing investigation on the $(\mathrm{Ni}_{2}\mathrm{Co}_{1})_{1-x}\mathrm{Fe}_{x}\mathrm{-MO}$ F-NFs and commercial $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ catalyst with a scan rate of $5\ \mathrm{mV\s^{-1}}$ , which could visually manifest the more outstanding OER activity of the MOF nanofoam than that of $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ . The detailed overpotential values at the current density of $10\ \mathrm{mA\cm^{-2}}$ are summarized in Figure  2B, where the minimum overpotential of $257~\mathrm{mV}$ is achieved in the $(\mathrm{Ni_{2}C o_{1}})_{0.925}\mathrm{Fe_{0.075}}\mathrm{-MOF-NF}$ . In addition, the LSV curves with different mass loadings are compared (Figure S8, Supporting Information), where $0.54~\mathrm{mg~cm}^{-2}$ is the optimal amount balancing the maximum exposure of active sites and the plateau effect.[1] In order to understand the electrochemical reaction kinetics, the corresponding Tafel plots are further analyzed (Figure  2C), among which the $(\mathrm{Ni_{2}C o_{1}})_{0.925}\mathrm{Fe_{0.075}}$ -MOF-NF delivers a much lower Tafel slope of $41.3~\mathrm{mV~dec^{-1}}$ compared to commercial $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ $(73.1\mathrm{mV}\mathrm{dec^{-1}})$ ), demonstrating its intrinsically favorable activity and kinetics. Furthermore, the electrochemically active surface areas (ECSAs) of these MOF catalysts are calculated based on the double-layer capacitances $(C_{\mathrm{dl}})$ through the cyclic voltammogram (CV) measurements (Figures S9 and 10, Supporting Information). The slopes of the different curves are in agreement with the trend of electrocatalytic activity for MOF nanofoam catalysts, among which the $(\mathrm{Ni_{2}C o_{1}})_{0.925}\mathrm{Fe_{0.075}}\mathrm{-MOF-NF}$ exhibits the highest value of $117.9\mathrm{mF}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ and largest specific surface area of $130.0~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ (Figure S11, Supporting Information), implying more active sites derived better performance associated with Fe contents. To further understand the OER reaction kinetics, their electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) are recorded at an applied potential of $1.53\mathrm{~V~}$ (vs RHE) from $100~\mathrm{kHz}$ to $0.01~\mathrm{Hz}$ (Figure  2D). The MOF nanofoam catalysts show two semicircles in the high- and low-frequency regions of Nyquist plots corresponding to the charge transfer resistance $(R_{\mathrm{ct}})$ and solution resistance $\left(R_{\mathrm{s}}\right)$ , respectively.[47,48]
Notably, the $(\mathrm{Ni_{2}C o_{1}})_{0.925}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.075}$ -MOF-NF possesses a smallest $R_{\mathrm{{ct}}}$ of $5.4~\Omega$ in contrast to other samples, which will definitely contribute to the optimized activity. Therefore, it can be concluded that the prominent activity of $(\mathrm{Ni_{2}C o_{1}})_{0.925}\mathrm{Fe_{0.075}–M O F–N F}$ could be derived from the synergistic effects of hierarchical foam-like morphology, favorable charge transfer kinetics and intrinsically more active sites.
As we know, Fe may have the positive role for OER since the doping of Fe may exert a partial-charge transfer activation effect on Ni and Co and improve conductivity.[39,49–52] In order to identify whether the OER activity of $(\mathrm{Ni}_{2}\mathrm{Co}_{1})_{1-x}\mathrm{Fe}_{x}.\mathrm{MOF}.\mathrm{NF}$ originates from Fe regardless the atomic ratio of Ni and Co, the counterpart of Fe-MOF is synthesized, which presents poor electrochemical performance with an overpotential of $326~\mathrm{mV}$ to reach the current density of $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ (Figure S12, Supporting Information). This result could further demonstrate that the enhanced OER activity may originate from the synergistic effect of the incorporated trimetal in the MOF structure. In addition, the influence of synthesis temperature is also investigated through the comparison study of samples synthesized at 50 and $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Figure S13, Supporting Information), where the OER activity decreases with the increasing temperatures (Figure S14, Supporting Information), implying the unique architecture formed at ambient temperature.
Long-term durability is also a crucial requirement for the practical applications of the electrocatalyst. To estimate the durability of the $(\mathrm{Ni_{2}C o_{1}})_{0.925}\mathrm{Fe_{0.075}}\mathrm{-MOF}\mathrm{-NF}.$ , the LSV curves after successive CV measurement between 1.2 and $1.55~\mathrm{~V~}$ versus RHE are recorded. It can be seen that the curves (Figure  3A) and the corresponding Tafel plots (Figure  3B) after different cycles remain quite stable compared to the initial cycle. After 3000 cycles, only a small overpotential increase of $8~\mathrm{mV}$ at the current density of $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ is observed (Figure  3C). Furthermore, the long-term chronoamperometry test at the overpotential of $258~\mathrm{mV}$ is conducted, where $96\%$ current density can be reserved after $35\mathrm{~h~}$ continuous test (Figure  3D), indicating the high durability of the electrocatalyst. In addition, other trimetallic $(\mathrm{Ni}_{2}\mathrm{Co}_{1})_{1-x}\mathrm{Fe}_{x}\mathrm{-MOF}$ -NFs also exhibit outstanding stability estimated by the repeated CV test (Figure S15, Supporting Information).
In order to unravel the possible origination of the outstanding OER activity in the trimetallic MOF nanofoams, we investigated the structural, chemical and electronic evolution process through the observation of the intermediates after different CV cycles. Figure 4A indicates the comparing LSV curves of initial and after 10, 50, 100, and $1000\mathrm{CV}$ cycles, respectively, among which each curve could coincide well with each other except the oxidation peak for $\mathrm{Ni^{2+}}/\mathrm{Ni^{3+}}$ in the initial cycle. However, their crystal structures go through the obvious variation during the first 100 cycles (Figure 4B). As can be seen, the XRD pattern collected after 10 cycles has already exhibited notable structural change compared with original MOF structure. Specifically, the original peaks belonged to MOF nanofoam become much weaker and several new peaks are observed, which could be ascribed to $\alpha{\mathrm{-Ni}}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}/\alpha{\mathrm{-Co}}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ (JCPDS No.38-0715) and NiOOH (JCPDS No.27-0956), respectively.[53,54] After 100 cycles, the MOF structure has completely disappeared while the pure hydroxide and oxyhydroxide remain stable even after 3000 cycles (Figure S16, Supporting Information). Furthermore, the corresponding ex-situ Raman spectra provide further evidence of the structural evolution (Figure  4C), where typical Raman vibrational peaks from the organic component of the pristine MOF structure at 1609, 1435, 1135, 862, and $633~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ disappear after 10 cycles, accompanying with the emergence of two weak and broad peaks at about 467 and $527\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ originated from the $\mathrm{A_{1g}}$ stretching modes of NiOH in the $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ and $\mathrm{e_{g}}$ bending vibration of $\mathrm{\DeltaNi-O}$ in the NiOOH.[51,55,56] The FTIR measurement also confirms the structural evolution where the absorption peaks at 3428, 1574, 1381, and $670{-}817\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ in the original MOF nanofoam (Figure S17, Supporting Information) could be assigned to the stretching vibrations of the O-H, asymmetric/ symmetric vibrations of the carboxyl groups and C-H bending vibrations of the benzene rings, respectively.[39,43,44] Importantly, two characteristic peaks for the MOF structure located at 3603 and $1505~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are completely eliminated after 100 cycles while two new absorption peaks at 534 and $470\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ derived from Ni–O vibration of ${\mathrm{Ni}}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ were presented.[57–59] The successive FTIR spectra after 1000 cycles remain identical, which is consistent with the XRD results.[60] The morphology of the intermediate after 1000 cycles (Figure 4D) indicates that the hierarchical porosity feature of the MOF nanofoam is inherited while interconnected nanofiber network was evolved to nanosheetlike assembly. The HRTEM analysis (Figure  4E) exhibits clear lattice fringes with spacing of about $0.25~\mathrm{{nm}}/0.24~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , which could be assigned to (101) planes of $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ phase and (002) planes of NiOOH. Moreover, the elemental mapping result (Figure  4F) confirms the existence and uniform distribution of Ni, Co, Fe, C, and O, indicating the unchanged composition although the notable variation of crystal structure during OER test. Furthermore, the XPS results are further analyzed to investigate the change of the chemical states of the MOF structure. First, the survey spectrum (Figure S18, Supporting Information) displays the existence of Ni, Co, Fe, C, and O elements, which is in agreement with the elemental mapping result. Importantly, the high-resolution spectrum of $\mathrm{Ni}\ 2\mathrm{p}$ (Figure  4G) clearly reflects that the peaks are shifted to lower binding energy (Ni $2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ at $855.4\ \mathrm{eV}$ and Ni $2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}$ at $872.9\ \mathrm{eV}$ ) after 100 cycles compared to pristine MOF nanofoam $\mathrm{(Ni~}2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ at $856.5\mathrm{eV}.$ , Ni $2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}$ at $874~\mathrm{eV}$ . This could be ascribed to destruction of the metal–organic ligand and the Ni atom would be directly connected with O atom, which will increase local electron density and thus result in lower binding energy of Ni 2p in the form of $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ .[61,62] More significantly, two new peaks derived from the $\mathrm{Ni}^{3+}$ with binding energy of 874.3 and $856.7\mathrm{eV}$ after 100 cycles are observed, respectively, which could further confirm the electrochemically formed NiOOH. Through the comparison of two fitted peaks of $\mathrm{Ni}~2\mathrm{p}$ after OER testing for 100 and 1000 cycles, there is no obvious distinction. Similarly, the $\mathrm{Co}\ 2\mathrm{p}$ peaks (Figure  4H) also exhibit the same variation trend, indicating the possible structural evolution from MOF to ${\mathrm{Co}}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ and CoOOH. In the case of Fe $2\mathrm{p}$ spectrum (Figure 4I), the binding energy of Fe $2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ in the pristine MOF nanofoam centered at $710\ \mathrm{eV}$ means the existence of $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ .[51] After 100 cycles, the binding energy of Fe $2\mathrm{p}_{2/3}$ shifts to $711\ \mathrm{eV}$ due to the structural evolution and keeps stable after 1000 cycles. The electronic structure change of O is also investigated (Figure 4J), where the dominant peaks of O 1s originated from $_{\mathrm{O-H}}$ $(533.6~\mathrm{eV})$ , $scriptstyle{\mathrm{O=C-O}}$ $(532.5~\mathrm{eV})$ , and $\scriptstyle\mathrm{M-O-R}$ $(531.5\ \mathrm{eV})$ distinctly shift to lower binding energy of $532/530.7\ \mathrm{eV}$ from $-\mathrm{OH/M{-}O{-}M}$ group, further demonstrating the possible formation of oxyhydroxide during water oxidation process.[63–66] Additionally, Fe $\mathrm{L}_{3,2}$ -edge XANES spectra (Figure 4K) indicates the notable shift to higher binding energy from $708.4\mathrm{eV}$ in the pristine MOF to $710.4\ \mathrm{eV}$ in the intermediate after 100 cycles, which keeps unchanged after 1000 cycles, demonstrating the decrease of electron density due to the change of coordination of Fe.[67,68] Moreover, XPS spectra of the intermediates after 1000, 2000, and $3000~\mathrm{CV}$ cycles remain consistent, indicating the unchanged chemical state after the initial 100 cycles, which is in agreement with the XRD and FTIR results (Figures S18 and S19, Supporting Information). Also, the chemical activation of $(\mathrm{Ni_{2}C o_{1}})_{0.925}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.075}$ -MOF-NF by immersing in 1 m KOH for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ can also transform pristine MOF structure to metal hydroxides while the OER activity is much inferior compared to electrochemically transformed samples (Figure S20, Supporting Information), which indicates the importance of the hierarchical foam-like structure as platform to assure the high activity.[69,70]
To summarize, we have developed a facile ambient temperature solution phase method for fast and large-scale synthesis of hierarchical foam-like trimetallic MOFs with modulated atomic ratios (denoted as $(\mathrm{Ni}_{2}\mathrm{Co}_{1})_{1-x}\mathrm{Fe}_{x}\mathrm{\mathrm{-}M O F\mathrm{-}N F}.$ ), which exhibit extraordinary electrocatalytic activity and outstanding durability when directly utilized as catalyst for water oxidation in alkaline condition. Specifically, the $(\mathrm{Ni_{2}C o_{1}})_{0.925}\mathrm{Fe_{0.075^{-}}}$ MOF-NF can deliver a minimum overpotential of $257~\mathrm{\mV}$ to reach the current density of $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ and a negligible activity attenuation can be observed after a long-term test. More importantly, the underlying origination of the high activity is deciphered through the investigation on the intermediates during the OER process, where it is found that the metal hydroxide and oxyhydroxide evolved from pristine MOF structure could be the active species while the hierarchical foam-like
MOF architecture provides an advanced platform to reach the morphology and composition optimization. We expect that this work will further stimulate the extensive attention on the exploration of unique MOF nanostructures with abundant active sites and excellent performance for potential electrocatalysis.
